# bridge.conf.example - the bridge's configuration file.
#
# One command-line token per line, WITHOUT the leading dash.  The file supplies OPTIONS;
# the command line supplies the MODE and its addresses, because those are positional
# (-initiator <ip> <port> <localip> / -target <ip> <port>) and a file that switched modes
# would silently change what was asked for.  A flag given on the command line wins over
# the file; -checkconfig validates this file without opening anything.
#
#   f5_interop.exe -initiator 192.168.100.5 4420 192.168.100.3 -config bridge.conf
#   f5_interop.exe -checkconfig bridge.conf

# --- the iSCSI side the bridge serves to Windows' own initiator
iscsi 3260
iscsiaddr 127.0.0.1
# Burst sizes: through Windows' initiator each SCSI command and each Data-Out PDU costs a
# fixed ~16-20 ms, so throughput is bought by putting more data behind each one.  These are
# the measured values; both are capped by the INITIATOR's registry (see the README).
iscsimbl 4194304
iscsichunk 1048576
# readwrite                      # serve the namespace read-write (read-only otherwise)
# iscsitime                      # per-command timing in the log (measurement, not production)

# --- connecting to the NVMe-oF target
subnqn nqn.2024-01.local.rdma:linux-nvmet
hostnqn nqn.2014-08.org.nvmexpress:uuid:ndvmeof-f5-0001
queues 4
backendretry 15                  # keep retrying instead of exiting when the peer is absent
# authkey "DHHC-1:01:....:"      # quotes, because the value ends with ':'
# reconnection is the TARGET side's option, not the initiator's

# --- operations
statusfile "C:\ProgramData\nvmeof-windows-nd\bridge-status.json"